@c ====================================================================
@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex introduction
@cindex Gas City
@cindex porcelain

Gas City is a system for running fleets of coding agents.  Its
@command{gc} command-line tool is the @dfn{plumbing}: it starts and
stops agents, routes work, and stores issues.  @code{gascity.el} is the
@dfn{porcelain}: a set of sectioned, keyboard-driven Emacs buffers that
render @command{gc}'s state and run its commands, in the deliberate style
of Magit and Forge.

@section Every view is a function of @command{gc} output

@cindex JSON
@cindex data plane
There is one rule behind the whole package: every view is a function of
@code{gc @dots{} --json} output.  A single module runs @command{gc},
decodes its JSON, and hands typed objects to the views; the views render
those objects and bind keys back to @command{gc} commands.  Nothing is
guessed --- what you see is what @command{gc} reports, and @kbd{g}
re-reads it.  gascity never talks to the Gas City supervisor's HTTP
API; where the CLI has no surface for something, the view leaves it
out (@pxref{Capability Gaps}).

@cindex store
@cindex payload store
Reads go through a per-city @dfn{store}: one cached payload per
@command{gc} command line, shared by every view of the city, with a
short freshness window per kind of read (five seconds for
@code{gc status}).  Opening a second view of the same city is instant,
two views asking for the same read cost one process, and a view keeps
showing its last good data while a refresh is in flight.

@cindex non-blocking
Nothing blocks Emacs.  Every read, and every action that needs no more
input from you, runs as a background process with a deadline; the
command you typed returns at once (@pxref{Actions}).

@section What you get

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{A cockpit} (@pxref{The Cockpit}) --- one short triage buffer
per city: what needs you, what is moving, who is working, what is
queued, what just happened, and the rigs.
@item
@strong{Agents and runs} (@pxref{Agents}, @pxref{Runs}) --- every
agent as a table or a tree, every workflow run with its step ladder,
and each of them in detail.
@item
@strong{A rig dashboard} (@pxref{The Rig Dashboard}) --- one rig, with
its agents, work, orders and Dolt.
@item
@strong{Live refresh} (@pxref{Live Refresh}) --- one event stream per
city; views refresh when something changes, never on a timer.
@item
@strong{City plumbing} (@pxref{Health}, @pxref{Cities}, @pxref{Costs})
--- the supervisor, versions, stores and @command{gc doctor}; every
city on every host; recorded usage.
@item
@strong{A mode-line lighter} (@pxref{The Mode-Line Lighter}) --- what
needs you, across every open city, in every mode line.
@item
@strong{Tabulated lists} (@pxref{The Lists}) --- rigs, sessions,
convoys, orders, mail and Dolt databases, each paginated, sortable and
filterable.
@item
@strong{At-point actions} (@pxref{Actions}) --- nudge, suspend, kill,
wake, drain, sling and more, run on the object under point in the
background and reported in the echo area.
@item
@strong{Remote cities} (@pxref{Remote Cities}) --- every view works
the same on a city opened over TRAMP.
@end itemize

@section Two kinds of buffer

@cindex vui
@cindex tabulated-list-mode
The porcelain is hand-built with two rendering technologies, chosen per
view:

@table @asis
@item vui
A small React-like reconciler drives the heterogeneous
@dfn{dashboards} --- the cockpit, the agent, run and rig detail views,
the Runs view and Health --- where sections of different shapes are stacked and
refreshed independently.
@item @code{tabulated-list-mode}
The built-in Emacs facility drives the homogeneous @dfn{lists}, where
every row has the same columns.
@end table

@cindex beads.el
Issue (@dfn{bead}) display itself is delegated to a sibling package,
@code{beads.el}: when you press @key{RET} on a bead, gascity opens it in
beads.el, scoped to the right rig's store.  gascity is the city-level
porcelain; beads.el is the issue-level porcelain.

@c ====================================================================
@node Installation
@chapter Installation
@cindex installation

@section Requirements

@cindex requirements
@cindex dependencies
@table @asis
@item GNU Emacs 29.1 or later
The porcelain uses facilities (e.g.@: @code{tabulated-list} truncation,
modern @code{transient}) that assume a recent Emacs.
@item The @command{gc} CLI on @code{exec-path}
gascity shells out to @command{gc} for every read and every action.  A
bare command name is looked up on @code{exec-path}; set
@code{gascity-executable} to use an absolute path (@pxref{Customization}).
@item @code{beads.el} and @code{vui} on the @code{load-path}
@code{beads.el} provides the @code{beads-meta}, @code{beads-section},
@code{beads-terminal} and @code{beads-pager} features that gascity builds
on; @code{vui} is the dashboard reconciler.
@item A running city (for live data)
With no reachable @command{gc}, a view never signals: each section whose
read failed shows one @samp{■ gc @dots{}: @var{reason}   g retry} line
and the others keep working.
@end table

@cindex vterm
@cindex eat
Optionally, install @code{vterm} or @code{eat} for a nicer terminal when
attaching to an agent's tmux session; without either, gascity falls back
to the built-in @code{term} (@pxref{Customization}).

The package header declares the full dependency set:

@example
(emacs "29.1") (transient "0.10.1") (vui "1.0.0")
(beads "0.1.0") (sesman "0.3.2")
@end example

@noindent
where @code{beads} is beads.el and @code{sesman} is pulled in
transitively through beads.el's terminal stack.

@section Loading from a checkout

@cindex loading
Put the @file{lisp} directory on your @code{load-path} and require the
entry point, which loads every module:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/gascity.el/lisp")
(require 'gascity)
@end lisp

@noindent
You are ready: run @kbd{M-x gascity-dashboard} (@pxref{Getting Started}).

@c ====================================================================
@node Getting Started
@chapter Getting Started
@cindex getting started
@cindex tour

@findex gascity-dashboard
The fastest way in is the cockpit.  With a city running, type

@example
M-x gascity-dashboard
@end example

@noindent
to open the city's cockpit: a header line with the city and whether it
refreshes live, two summary lines, then six short sections --- Needs
you, Moving, Agents, Work, Activity and Rigs.

Move from thing to thing with @key{TAB} and @kbd{S-@key{TAB}}, toggle
the thing at point with @key{SPC} (fold a section, expand a fold row,
open a row's inline detail), act on it with @key{RET}, and reload with
@kbd{g}.  @kbd{?} lists every verb under the key it has in the views and
@kbd{j} jumps to another view.  These keys are shared by every gascity
buffer; they are described once in @ref{Buffers and Navigation}.

@section The dispatcher

@findex gascity
@cindex dispatcher
@cindex transient
Outside a gascity buffer, @kbd{M-x gascity} raises a command-dispatch
menu: the left columns open the porcelain views --- @kbd{h} the
cockpit, the @strong{Lists} column the tabulated lists --- and the
@strong{Dispatch} column routes the mutating actions, prompting for
their arguments (@pxref{Actions}).  Inside a view, @kbd{?} is the
better menu: it lists every verb under the key it has in the views
(@pxref{The ? dispatch}).

@cindex entry points
@noindent
The view-opening commands, all interactive, are:

@table @code
@item gascity-dashboard
The city cockpit (@pxref{The Cockpit}).
@item gascity-rig-dashboard
A single rig in detail; prompts for the rig (@pxref{The Rig Dashboard}).
@item gascity-agents
Every agent of the city; @kbd{T} for the tree (@pxref{Agents}).
@item gascity-runs
Workflow runs (@pxref{Runs}).
@item gascity-health
Supervisor, versions, stores and @command{gc doctor} (@pxref{Health}).
@item gascity-cities
Every city on this machine and the remote hosts (@pxref{Cities}).
@item gascity-costs
@command{gc costs} output (@pxref{Costs}).
@item gascity-rig-list
@itemx gascity-session-list
@itemx gascity-convoy-list
@itemx gascity-mail-inbox
@itemx gascity-order-list
@itemx gascity-dolt-list
The six tabulated lists (@pxref{The Lists}).
@item gascity-mode-line-mode
The global, opt-in mode-line lighter (@pxref{The Mode-Line Lighter}).
@item gascity
The dispatcher transient.
@end table

@c ====================================================================
@node Buffers and Navigation
@chapter Buffers and Navigation
@cindex navigation
@cindex section model

Every gascity buffer is read-only and keyboard-driven, and one set of
keys means the same thing in all of them: the vui views (cockpit,
detail views, Health) and the tabulated lists alike.  This chapter
documents those keys once; each later chapter adds only the keys unique
to its view.

@menu
* Moving and toggling::   TAB, S-TAB, SPC, RET, N, P and friends.
* The jump prefix::       @kbd{j}: go to another view.
* The ? dispatch::        Every verb under its view key.
* Filter menus::          @kbd{/} and its letters.
* Visual language::       Glyphs, times and section states.
* Objects at point::      What an action acts on.
* Lists::                 Pagination, sorting, the detail window.
@end menu

@node Moving and toggling
@section Moving and toggling

@cindex sections
@cindex things
@cindex collapsing
@cindex drawer
The vui views are built from @dfn{sections}: a header line followed by
its rows.  A @dfn{thing} is every line that carries something you can
act on: a section header, a row whose object is an agent, session,
bead, run, rig or event, a fold row (@samp{▸ stopped @dots{}}, a pool,
@samp{▸ 88 checks ok}), and a @samp{@dots{} N more} line.  The
summary lines at the top, blank lines and dim status lines are
decoration and are skipped.

@table @kbd
@item n
@kindex n
@findex next-line
Move down one line.
@item p
@kindex p
@findex previous-line
Move up one line.
@item N
@kindex N
@findex gascity-section-next
Jump to the next section header (the coarse counterpart to @kbd{n}).
@item P
@kindex P
@findex gascity-section-previous
Jump to the previous section header.
@item @key{TAB}
@itemx S-@key{TAB}
@kindex TAB
@kindex S-TAB
@findex gascity-thing-forward
@findex gascity-thing-backward
Move to the next (previous) thing.  At the last thing the motion wraps
to the first and echoes @samp{Wrapped}, as @code{forward-button} does.
@kbd{<tab>}, @kbd{<backtab>} and @kbd{S-<tab>} are bound too, so the
keys work in GUI frames and terminals alike.
@item @key{SPC}
@kindex SPC
@findex gascity-thing-toggle
Toggle the thing at point: on a section header, fold or unfold the
section; on a fold row, expand or collapse it in place; on any other
row, open or close its inline @dfn{drawer} --- a few dim @samp{│} lines
of detail read from data already loaded (no @command{gc} call).  On a
line with nothing to toggle it says so.  Folds and drawers are view
state: they survive a refresh.
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET
Act on the thing at point --- the precise meaning depends on the view
(attach an agent's terminal, open a bead, open a run).  On a section
header it opens the section's full view.  @key{RET} never folds.
@item q
@kindex q
@findex quit-window
Bury the buffer.
@item g
@kindex g
Reload the view from @command{gc}, preserving point, folds and drawers.
@item ?
@kindex ?
The dispatch menu (@pxref{The ? dispatch}).
@item j
@kindex j
The jump prefix (@pxref{The jump prefix}).
@item /
@kindex /
The view's filter menu (@pxref{Filter menus}).
@end table

@noindent
@kbd{DEL} and @kbd{S-@key{SPC}} are deliberately unbound, since
@key{SPC} no longer scrolls; @kbd{C-v} and @kbd{M-v} still do, and the
views are short by design.

@cindex nudge, mnemonic
@cindex peek, mnemonic
@quotation Note
Because @kbd{N} and @kbd{P} take the section-motion keys and @kbd{p}
takes previous-line, the two actions that once lived there moved: agent
@dfn{nudge} is now @kbd{M} (``message'') and @dfn{peek} is now @kbd{v}.
@end quotation

@node The jump prefix
@section The jump prefix @kbd{j}

@cindex jump prefix
@kindex j
@findex gascity-jump-cockpit
@findex gascity-jump-agents
@findex gascity-jump-beads
@findex gascity-jump-health
@findex gascity-jump-cities
@findex gascity-jump-costs
@findex gascity-jump-rig
Views are reached through @kbd{j} followed by one letter, in every
gascity view.  (Single capitals are taken: @kbd{R}, for one, is
reset.)

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Keys @tab View
@item @kbd{j j} @tab the cockpit (@pxref{The Cockpit})
@item @kbd{j a} @tab agents (@pxref{Agents})
@item @kbd{j r} @tab runs (@pxref{Runs})
@item @kbd{j b} @tab beads in beads.el, scoped to the rig at point, else chosen: a rig or the city
@item @kbd{j m} @tab the mail inbox (@pxref{Mail View})
@item @kbd{j e} @tab events (@pxref{Events})
@item @kbd{j h} @tab Health (@pxref{Health})
@item @kbd{j c} @tab Cities (@pxref{Cities})
@item @kbd{j o} @kbd{j v} @kbd{j d} @tab the order, convoy and Dolt lists (@pxref{The Lists})
@item @kbd{j g} @tab the dashboard of the rig at point, else chosen
@item @kbd{j $} @tab @command{gc costs} output (@pxref{Costs})
@end multitable

@noindent
The rig prompts of @kbd{j g} and @kbd{j b} complete over the rigs
gascity already knows and refresh that list in the background; they
never wait on @command{gc}.  A jump to a view that is not built yet
echoes so instead of failing.

@node The ? dispatch
@section The @kbd{?} dispatch

@cindex dispatch transient
@kindex ?
@findex gascity-dispatch
@kbd{?} opens @code{gascity-dispatch}, a transient menu of every verb
and view.  Each suffix sits under the key it has in the views, so the
menu teaches the view keys:

@table @asis
@item Jump
@kbd{j j} @dots{} @kbd{j $}, as above.
@item Agent at point
@kbd{M} nudge, @kbd{s} suspend, @kbd{w} wake, @kbd{K} kill, @kbd{D}
drain, @kbd{R} reset, @kbd{U} undrain, @kbd{t} attach.
@item Work
@kbd{S} sling, @kbd{c} bead actions, @kbd{m} mail actions.
@item City
@kbd{L} reload, @kbd{C} city lifecycle (start, stop, suspend), @kbd{O}
run an order, @kbd{W} toggle live refresh, @kbd{g} refresh, @kbd{/}
filter.
@end table

@noindent
Its heading names the city and its path.

@shot{dispatch-dark, The ? dispatch over the cockpit (ef-elea-dark)}

@node Filter menus
@section Filter menus

@cindex filter
@kindex /
@kbd{/} opens the view's filter menu.  Every change applies at once
(there is no ``apply'' step), @kbd{x} resets every filter, and one
letter means one thing in every menu:

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .48 .40
@headitem Key @tab Meaning @tab Views
@item @kbd{-w} @kbd{-n} @kbd{-o} @kbd{-m} @tab show wisps / nudge beads / order churn / message beads @tab cockpit
@item @kbd{-c} @tab unfold churn @tab cockpit, Events
@item @kbd{-r} @tab rig@dots{} @tab cockpit, Agents, Runs, sessions
@item @kbd{-W} @tab time window@dots{} @tab cockpit, Runs, Events, Mail
@item @kbd{-s} @tab state@dots{} @tab Agents, Runs, sessions, rigs, convoys
@item @kbd{-q} @tab search@dots{} @tab Agents, Events, Mail
@item @kbd{-t} @tab event type@dots{} @tab Events
@item @kbd{-l} @tab signal level@dots{} @tab Events
@item @kbd{-a} @tab actor / sender@dots{} @tab Events, Mail
@item @kbd{-p} @tab provider@dots{} @tab Agents
@item @kbd{-F} @tab formula@dots{} @tab Runs
@item @kbd{-u} @tab unread only @tab Mail
@item @kbd{-e} @kbd{-t} @tab enabled only / type@dots{} @tab orders
@item @kbd{-S} @tab sort by@dots{} (across all pages) @tab every list
@item @kbd{x} @tab reset all @tab every menu
@end multitable


@node Visual language
@section Visual language

@cindex glyphs
Every view uses the same glyphs, aligned with the Gas City web
dashboard:

@multitable @columnfractions .1 .9
@headitem Glyph @tab Meaning
@item @samp{●} @tab ok, running, active
@item @samp{○} @tab stopped or idle (not a problem)
@item @samp{▲} @tab watch (a warning)
@item @samp{■} @tab attention: stuck, failed, down
@item @samp{◐} @tab partial or stale data; the reason is in the tooltip
@item @samp{⬣} @tab run step active
@item @samp{◆} @tab run step done
@item @samp{·} @tab run step pending
@item @samp{✕} @tab run step failed
@item @samp{▸} @samp{▾} @tab a fold row, collapsed / expanded
@item @samp{@dots{}} @tab loading, or an action in flight on this row
@end multitable

@cindex relative times
Times are relative (@samp{3m}, @samp{2h}, @samp{yesterday},
@samp{Sep 22}) with the ISO timestamp as a tooltip.

@cindex section states
Every section shows its load the same way:

@table @asis
@item loading, no data yet
@samp{@dots{}} in the header.
@item read failed, no data
One line, @samp{■ gc status: @var{first line of the error}   g retry}.
@item refresh failed over good data
The rows stay; the header gets @samp{◐ stale}, or @samp{◐ timed out}
when the read hit its deadline, with the reason as a tooltip.
@item empty
@samp{none} in the header.
@end table

@noindent
A host that cannot be reached at all is shown once, as @samp{○ offline
@@host} in the view's header, not as an error in every section
(@pxref{Remote Cities}).  Views say what they hide --- @samp{(98 wisps
hidden)} --- and never print a line apologizing for data @command{gc}
does not provide.

@node Objects at point
@section Objects at point

@cindex object at point
Actions resolve the @dfn{object at point} through one precedence ladder:
an agent, then a bead id, then the current list row, then the buffer's
subject (the agent a detail view is @emph{about}).  This is why an action
key works whether point is on a specific row or anywhere in a detail view
that has a single subject.

@node Lists
@section Lists: pagination, sorting, detail

@cindex pagination
@cindex sorting
The tabulated lists size each page to the window and carry these keys in
addition to the standard @code{tabulated-list-mode} bindings (column
sorting on the header line, and so on):

@table @kbd
@item ]
@kindex ]
@findex gascity-tabulated-next-page
Next page.
@item [
@kindex [
@findex gascity-tabulated-prev-page
Previous page.
@item G
@kindex G
@findex gascity-tabulated-goto-page
Jump to a page number (prompts).
@item S
@kindex S
Sling (the same in every view); sort with a header click or @kbd{/ -S}
instead, which sorts across @emph{all} pages.
@item @key{SPC}
@kindex SPC @r{(lists)}
@findex gascity-tabulated-detail-toggle
Show the row at point in a @file{*gascity-detail*} side window, or close
it.  @kbd{C-o} shows it without selecting it and @kbd{C-c C-f} makes it
follow point; @kbd{q} closes it before burying the list.
@item /
@kindex /
Open the list's filter menu: changes apply at once, @kbd{x} resets,
@kbd{-S} sorts.
@end table

@noindent
The mode line shows @samp{Name [page/total] (filter)}; an over-long cell
is truncated to its column with an ellipsis and a @code{help-echo}
tooltip carrying the full value.

@c ====================================================================
@node The Cockpit
@chapter The Cockpit
@cindex cockpit
@findex gascity-dashboard

@kbd{M-x gascity-dashboard} opens the city's cockpit in
@code{gascity-dashboard-mode}: one short triage buffer answering, top to
bottom, what needs you, what is moving, who is working, what is queued
and what just happened.  No section shows more than
@code{gascity-dashboard-section-rows} rows (five); the rest is a
@samp{@dots{} N more} line whose @key{RET} opens the full view.

@shot{cockpit-dark, The cockpit of emacs-city (ef-elea-dark)}

@section What it shows

The header line names the city (with @samp{@@host} for a remote one),
the state of its live event stream (@pxref{Live Refresh}) and how long
ago it last refreshed.  The
buffer opens with the city and its path, then one summary line: agents
running out of total, live sessions, active and failed runs, ready
beads, unread mail and the beads store's size (@samp{◐} when
@command{gc} reports partial errors, with the reason as a tooltip).

@table @asis
@item Needs you
Stalled agents (running without a live session), sessions that crashed
or timed out and were not woken since, runs with no step progress for
@code{gascity-dashboard-run-idle-threshold} seconds, runs that failed,
beads reopened after their assignee died, beads labelled
@samp{gc:escalation} or @samp{hold:*}, unread mail and store-health
warnings --- @samp{■} before @samp{▲}.
@item Moving
Active runs with their step ladder (@samp{◆} done, @samp{⬣} active,
@samp{·} pending, @samp{✕} failed) and their live workers; in-progress
work outside a run is listed flat.
@item Agents
Running agents by recent activity, then idle ones; stopped agents fold
into one @samp{▸ stopped} line.  Named sessions such as the mayor are
agents too.
@item Work
Ready beads by priority, then age.  Wisps, nudge beads, order churn,
message beads and session beads are hidden, always with a
@samp{(N hidden)} count.
@item Activity
Recent events (@code{gascity-dashboard-window}, two hours by default).
Order firings, noise-bead lifecycle and bead updates fold into
@samp{×N} rows per 15 minutes; signal events never fold.
@item Rigs
One row per rig: prefix, path, agents running, work in progress.
@end table

Sections show their load state the common way (@pxref{Visual
language}).  An agent row with an action in flight shows @samp{@dots{}}
in place of its glyph until @command{gc} answers.

@cindex Needs you totals
Every time the cockpit renders with a status payload in hand it
publishes its Needs you @samp{■}/@samp{▲} totals and active-run count
for the session; the mode-line lighter (@pxref{The Mode-Line Lighter})
and the Cities view (@pxref{Cities}) show them from there, without a
@command{gc} call of their own.

@shot{cockpit-light, The same cockpit in a light theme (ef-cyprus)}

