@node Cities
@chapter Cities
@cindex cities
@findex gascity-cities
@kindex j c

@kbd{j c} (or @kbd{M-x gascity-cities}) lists every city gascity can
reach, one row each, in @file{*gascity-cities*}:

@example
 City               Where                              Agents Runs Mail Health
 ● bright-lights    ~/bright-lights/                   2/4         ▲4   ●
 ● emacs-city       ~/emacs-city/                      1/5    1 ⬣  ▲5   ▲
 ● bright-lights    /ssh:localhost:~/bright-lights/    2/4         ▲4   ●
@end example

@shot{cities-light, The Cities view (ef-cyprus)}

@cindex hosts, Cities view
@vindex gascity-remote-hosts
The rows come from @code{gc cities} run on each host: the local one,
every host in @code{gascity-remote-hosts}, and every remote host a
gascity view has read from this session --- visiting a remote city is
enough to add its host.  (@command{gc}'s own
@file{~/.gc/contexts.toml} is not used: it registers HTTPS cities for
@code{gc --context}, not ssh hosts.)

Each row fills in from that city's own @code{gc status} (Agents running
out of total, the City glyph and Health) and @code{gc mail count}
(unread mail, @samp{▲N}).  The rows follow the store, so when the
city's cockpit refreshes, its row updates too.  Runs shows the active
runs of a city whose cockpit is open, and is blank otherwise, because
no single @command{gc} read gives it.  Where is host-qualified.  A host
that cannot be reached is one @samp{○ offline} row, updated when the
host comes back; a host whose @code{gc cities} failed is one @samp{■}
row with the error.

@table @kbd
@item @key{RET}
@kindex RET @r{(cities)}
@findex gascity-cities-visit
Open the cockpit of the city at point, on its host.
@item g
@kindex g @r{(cities)}
@findex gascity-cities-refresh
Re-read every host and city.
@end table

