@c ====================================================================
@node Live Refresh
@chapter Live Refresh
@cindex live refresh
@cindex event stream
@findex gascity-live-toggle
@kindex W

Views don't poll.  While at least one view of a city is open, the city
runs @emph{one} @code{gc events --follow} stream; events are collected
for @code{gascity-live-debounce} seconds (2.5) and then only the
sections they affect re-read: a session or agent event refreshes the
agent sections, a bead event the work and run sections, a mail event
the mail counts, and so on.  The last view of a city to close stops its
stream.

The header line shows the stream's state:

@table @samp
@item ● live
The stream is running.
@item ○ live off
You turned it off with @kbd{W}; @kbd{g} still refreshes.
@item ○ live: reconnecting (@var{N}s)
The stream dropped; it reconnects after 2, 5, 15, then every 60 seconds
(@code{gascity-live-backoff}), resuming after the last event it saw, so
nothing is missed, and then refreshes everything once.  @kbd{g}
reconnects at once.
@item ○ live: supervisor down
@command{gc} serves events from the supervisor.  With it down the
stream and the event reads fail, while every other section keeps
working; @kbd{g} still refreshes.
@item ○ offline @@host
The host cannot be reached (@pxref{Remote Cities}).
@end table

@noindent
The stream's error output goes to @file{*gascity-live:@: CITY*}; its
last line is the reason shown in the header.

@kbd{W} (@code{gascity-live-toggle}) turns the stream off or on for the
city; @code{gascity-live-enabled} sets the default.  A remote city's
stream runs as a local @code{ssh -T} pipe, never on a TRAMP channel.
For remote methods plain ssh cannot reach (docker, sudo, multi-hop),
gascity polls @code{gc events} every @code{gascity-live-poll-interval}
seconds (30) instead.

