@c ====================================================================
@node Actions
@chapter Actions
@cindex actions
@cindex mutating commands

Beyond reading, gascity dispatches @command{gc}'s mutating commands.
Every action is reachable two ways: @dfn{at point} (a key acts on the
rig, agent, mail or order under point) and @dfn{by prompt} (from the
dispatcher's sub-transients or @kbd{M-x}, reading arguments with
completion).

@section Actions never block

@cindex asynchronous actions
@cindex pending action
@cindex @file{*gascity-log*}
An action gathers what it needs from you first --- a confirmation, a
nudge message in the minibuffer, a transient, a compose buffer --- and
then only @emph{starts} the @command{gc} call and returns.  You can keep
working while it runs:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{Pending.} The row the action is about shows @samp{@dots{}} in
its status slot (the glyph of a vui row, the status column of a list)
until @command{gc} answers.
@item
@strong{Success.} The echo area says what happened ---
@samp{Suspended beads.el/requirements-planner-1} --- and the views the
action touched re-read.
@item
@strong{Failure.} The echo area shows the first line of @command{gc}'s
error; the full error goes to the city's
@file{*gascity-log:@: CITY*} buffer (@file{*gascity-log:@:
CITY@@host*} for a remote city).  A failure never pops up a modal
error.
@item
@strong{Ordering.} Actions on the same target run one after the other;
actions on different targets run at the same time.  Killing a view
does not cancel an action in flight: it still finishes and reports.
@item
@strong{Deadline.} Every call is killed after
@code{gascity-remote-async-timeout} seconds (@pxref{Remote Cities}),
locally as well as remotely.
@end itemize

@noindent
Destructive actions --- kill, reset, rig restart, city start and stop,
@command{gc doctor --fix} --- confirm first; the quick ones (nudge,
suspend, wake, drain, undrain) don't.  City start and stop keep a
buffer streaming their long-running output.

@section Session actions

@cindex nudge
@cindex suspend
@cindex kill
@cindex drain
@cindex peek
Run at point in the dashboards and the session list, or by prompt from
@kbd{A} in the dispatcher (@code{gascity-session-dispatch}):

@table @asis
@item Nudge --- @kbd{M}
@code{gc session nudge}: send the agent a message (prompts for it).  The
ephemeral, zero-overhead way to signal an agent.
@item Suspend --- @kbd{s}
@code{gc session suspend}: stop the agent's runtime; its bead persists.
@item Kill --- @kbd{K}
@code{gc session kill}: force-kill the runtime (the reconciler may
restart it).  Confirms first.
@item Wake --- @kbd{w}
@code{gc session wake}: wake a suspended agent.
@item Drain --- @kbd{D}
@code{gc runtime drain}: wind the agent down gracefully --- it finishes
its current task, then exits.  Distinct from suspend and kill.
@item Undrain --- @kbd{U}
@code{gc runtime undrain}: cancel a drain.
@item Reset --- @kbd{R}
@code{gc session reset}: restart the agent's session fresh.  Confirms first.
@item Peek --- @kbd{v}
@code{gc session peek}: show the agent's recent terminal output in a
read-only buffer.
@end table

@section Rig actions

By prompt from @kbd{R} in the dispatcher (@code{gascity-rig-dispatch}), or
at point in the rig list:

@table @asis
@item Suspend --- @kbd{s}
@code{gc rig suspend}.
@item Resume --- @kbd{r}
@code{gc rig resume}.
@item Restart --- @kbd{R}
@code{gc rig restart}.  Confirms, as it kills the rig's agent sessions.
@end table

@section Dispatch and lifecycle

@cindex sling
@cindex order
@cindex city lifecycle
@table @asis
@item Sling --- dispatcher @kbd{S}
@findex gascity-sling
@code{gc sling}: route a bead id, a formula, or free task text to an
agent.  Supports a formula, a nudge, and a dry run.
@item Run order --- dispatcher @kbd{O}, list @kbd{x}
@findex gascity-order-run
@code{gc order run}: run an order now, bypassing its trigger.
@item City lifecycle --- dispatcher @kbd{L}
@findex gascity-start
@findex gascity-stop
@code{gc start} and @code{gc stop} start and stop the whole city.  Both
confirm first and stream their (long-running) output; a prefix argument to
stop forces a hard, graceless shutdown.
@end table

@section Attached terminals

@cindex attach
@cindex scrolling a terminal
@cindex copy-mode
@kbd{t} on an agent (@code{gascity-tmux-at-point}) opens a terminal buffer
holding a live @command{tmux} client.  A tmux client draws on the
@dfn{alternate screen}, so the Emacs buffer contains only what is on
screen right now: the history is tmux's, and the backend's own scrollback
and copy commands have nothing to show.  Scrolling therefore goes through
tmux:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-b [}
Enter tmux's copy-mode.  @kbd{C-b} is the prefix on Gas City's per-city
tmux servers; a personal @file{~/.tmux.conf} elsewhere may rebind it.
@item @key{PageUp} / @key{PageDown}
Page through the history once in copy-mode --- and @kbd{C-b @key{PageUp}}
enters copy-mode a page back in one go.  @kbd{M-v} and @kbd{C-v} do the
same, and the arrow keys move a line at a time.
@item @kbd{q}
Leave copy-mode and return to the live pane.
@end table

@cindex bead at point, terminal
@findex gascity-bead-show-at-point
Agent transcripts are full of bead ids.  Put point on one --- the
backend's own copy mode, @kbd{C-c C-t} in vterm, frees point from the
terminal cursor --- and Eldoc shows the bead's status and title
(beads.el's @code{beads-eldoc-mode}, looked up asynchronously and scoped
to the rig owning the id's prefix), while @kbd{C-c b}
(@code{gascity-bead-show-at-point}) opens it in beads.el's detail view.
The same command works in any text buffer, a shell or a mail body
included.

These keys only reach tmux because gascity suppresses the global minor
modes named in @code{gascity-terminal-unshadow-minor-modes} inside its
terminal buffers; a minor-mode map outranks the terminal backend's own
keymap, so without that, @code{pixel-scroll-precision-mode} (for
instance) swallows @key{PageUp} and @key{PageDown} and scrolls the Emacs
window --- which has nothing above the visible screen to scroll to.

The mouse wheel is a tmux setting, not an Emacs one: it scrolls the
history only where the city's tmux server runs with @code{mouse on}
(@code{set -g mouse on}), which is not the default.  How far back you can
scroll is tmux's @code{history-limit} --- 2000 lines unless the city says
otherwise.

@c ====================================================================
@node Remote Cities
@chapter Remote Cities
@cindex remote cities
@cindex TRAMP

Every view works the same on a city on another host.  Open any
directory of the city over TRAMP and start a view from there; every
read, action and refresh then runs @command{gc} on that host, never a
silent local fallback:

@example
C-x d /ssh:user@@example.com:/home/user/city/ @key{RET}
M-x gascity-dashboard
@end example

@menu
* Remote buffers::        Host-qualified names, paths, attach.
* Transport::             How gc runs on the host: local ssh pipes.
* Scheduling::            Concurrency, deadlines, offline hosts.
* Finding gc::            Resolving the executable on the host.
* ssh configuration::     ForwardX11 and connection sharing.
* Shell history::         Keeping remote history files clean.
@end menu

@node Remote buffers
@section Remote buffers

@itemize @bullet
@item
Buffer names are host-qualified
(@file{*gascity:@: city@@/ssh:user@@example.com:/home/user/city/*}), so a
local and a remote view of the same city coexist; the header line shows
@samp{@@host}.  Each view pins its @code{default-directory} to the city
it was opened for, so refreshes keep hitting that host.
@item
Paths @command{gc} reports (agent worktrees, rig directories, order
sources, the store) are host-local; @kbd{d} (Dired), @kbd{l} (git log)
and @key{RET} re-prefix them and open them on the city's host.
@item
Rendering, redisplay, timers, tooltips, @key{TAB} and @key{SPC} use
only data already in memory: no view touches the remote host except
through its explicit reads.  Home-directory abbreviation (@samp{~/}) on
a remote path is pure string work.
@item
@kbd{t} and @key{RET} tmux attach spawn a @emph{local}
@code{ssh -t HOST env -u TMUX tmux @dots{}} in the terminal backend.
Supported for the ssh-based TRAMP methods (@code{ssh}, @code{sshx},
@code{scp}, @code{scpx}); other methods and multi-hop names signal a
clear error.
@end itemize

@node Transport
@section Transport: local ssh pipes

@cindex ssh pipe
@cindex transport
@vindex gascity-remote-transport
@vindex gascity-remote-ssh-options
For a single-hop ssh-family city, every background read and action runs
as a @emph{local} @code{ssh -T} process with pipes, not as a TRAMP
process.  Starting one never blocks Emacs (a TRAMP @code{make-process}
sets up a remote shell synchronously, about half a second of frozen
Emacs per read), its output is byte-exact (no pty) and its errors arrive
separately.  ssh runs with @code{BatchMode=yes}, so it never prompts:
key or agent authentication is required.  The processes share one ssh
master of their own (a ControlPath distinct from TRAMP's), so only the
first pays for the handshake.

@defvr {User Option} gascity-remote-transport
@code{ssh} (default): the local ssh pipe described above.
@code{tramp}: always use TRAMP's @code{make-process}.  Other TRAMP
methods (docker, sudo, multi-hop) always use TRAMP.
@end defvr

@noindent
The live event stream (@pxref{Live Refresh}) runs the same way, as one
long-lived local ssh pipe per city.

@defvr {User Option} gascity-remote-ssh-options
Extra @command{ssh} options for the pipe processes (default: a
ControlMaster with @code{ControlPersist=60}).  gascity always adds
@code{-T}, @code{-n}, @code{-o BatchMode=yes} and
@code{-o ForwardX11=no}.
@end defvr

@node Scheduling
@section Scheduling, deadlines and offline hosts

@cindex scheduler
@cindex deadline
@cindex offline host
Reads and actions are scheduled per host, in two lanes so that actions
never queue behind reads.  A view in a visible window is served first.

@defvr {User Option} gascity-remote-max-inflight
At most this many @command{gc} processes run at once per remote host
and lane (default 3); the rest wait in a queue.  Local cities are not
capped.  @code{nil} means unlimited.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-remote-async-timeout
Seconds before a background @command{gc} process is killed (default
30), locally as well as remotely: a wedged @command{gc} must never hold
a view forever.  A read that times out keeps its section's last good
data, marked @samp{◐ timed out}.  @command{gc doctor} has its own,
longer deadline, @code{gascity-health-doctor-timeout}.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-remote-sync-timeout
Seconds before a @emph{synchronous} remote call is abandoned (default
30, 0 disables): the few calls Emacs itself makes synchronously --- a
Dired or git log you asked for, and the first contact with a host.
@end defvr

@vindex gascity-store-offline-backoff
When a read fails because the host cannot be reached, the whole city
flips to @samp{○ offline @@host} in the view's header --- not an error
in every section --- the host's queue pauses, and one probe is retried
after 2, 5, 15, then every 60 seconds (@code{gascity-store-offline-backoff}).
The Cities view shows such a host as one @samp{○ offline} row.

@vindex gascity-store-ttl-alist
Payloads are shared by every view of a city and fresh for a few seconds
per kind of read (@code{gascity-store-ttl-alist}: @code{gc status} five
seconds, @code{gc bd} reads fifteen, doctor until you run it again).
@kbd{g} always re-reads.

@node Finding gc
@section Finding @command{gc} on the host

@cindex executable resolution
@vindex beads-remote-search-path
Setup is usually none.  A bare program name is resolved on the host ---
first against @code{tramp-remote-path}, then, when that misses, by
probing the profile directories in @code{beads-remote-search-path}
(shared with beads.el): the Guix profiles (@file{~/.guix-home/profile/bin},
@file{~/.guix-profile/bin}, @file{/run/current-system/profile/bin}),
then Nix, @file{~/.local/bin} and @file{~/bin}.  Resolutions are cached
per connection; @kbd{M-x gascity-context-clear-cache} forgets them.
@command{tmux} is resolved the same way.  Every remote invocation also
puts those directories on @env{PATH}, so @command{gc}'s own children
(@command{git}, @command{dolt}) resolve too.

For other layouts, extend TRAMP's search path:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'tramp-remote-path 'tramp-own-remote-path)
@end lisp

@noindent
or set a per-host absolute path with connection-local variables:

@lisp
(connection-local-set-profile-variables
 'gascity-remote-gc
 '((gascity-executable . "/home/user/.guix-home/profile/bin/gc")))
(connection-local-set-profiles
 '(:machine "example.com") 'gascity-remote-gc)
@end lisp

@node ssh configuration
@section ssh configuration

@cindex ForwardX11
@cindex @code{tramp-use-connection-share}
@cindex wedge, TRAMP
Two ssh settings can freeze Emacs over TRAMP.  gascity's own ssh pipes
avoid both, but TRAMP's connection (Dired, the first contact with a
host) does not, so check your setup:

@table @asis
@item @code{ForwardX11 yes}
With X11 forwarding on for the host (often via a @code{Host *} block in
@file{~/.ssh/config}), the ssh master prints @samp{Warning: No xauth
data; @dots{}} into TRAMP's connection for every multiplexed session.
When those warnings land after TRAMP's prompt, TRAMP never sees the
prompt and spins.  Turn X11 forwarding off for the hosts you open
cities on:

@example
Host build-box
    ForwardX11 no
@end example

@noindent
or for TRAMP only:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'tramp-sh
  (dolist (method '("ssh" "sshx" "scp" "scpx"))
    (let ((args (assq 'tramp-login-args
                      (cdr (assoc method tramp-methods)))))
      (when (and args
                 (not (member '("-o" "ForwardX11=no") (cadr args))))
        (setcar (cdr args)
                (cons '("-o" "ForwardX11=no") (cadr args)))))))
@end lisp

@item TRAMP connection sharing
TRAMP runs its processes as clients of one ssh ControlMaster, over
ptys.  The master writes each client's output into its pty with
blocking writes; while TRAMP waits (without a timeout) for one
command, a large output on another pty can fill it and block the
master, so the awaited command is never forwarded.  If a remote city
ever freezes Emacs inside a TRAMP wait, stop TRAMP from sharing the
connection:

@lisp
(setq tramp-use-connection-share 'suppress)
@end lisp

@noindent
This costs one full ssh handshake per TRAMP connection.
@end table

@node Shell history
@section Shell history on the host

@subheading The host's @file{~/.bashrc}

@cindex TRAMP, host shell history pollution
@cindex shell history guard
@cindex @file{~/.bashrc}
Every connection TRAMP opens spawns a login shell on the remote host, and
@command{sshd} sources @file{~/.bashrc} for non-interactive commands too
(on many setups even @kbd{ssh host true} appends a line to
@file{~/.bash_history}).  When @file{~/.bashrc} contains a multi-window
@code{PROMPT_COMMAND} history trick (an @code{shopt -s histappend} block
that runs @code{history -a} after every prompt), each of those shells also
writes the login command into the history files.  With gascity.el's
auto-refreshing remote views this can mean an entry per refresh tick.
gascity.el does not write the history itself; this is host-side shell
behavior, so the fix lives in your @file{~/.bashrc} and works regardless
of gascity.el.

Put the following guard at the very top of @file{~/.bashrc} on the host,
before anything else --- before profile sourcing and in particular before
the @code{PROMPT_COMMAND} history block:

@example
# Non-interactive shells spawned over ssh (one-shot @command{ssh}
# commands, Emacs/TRAMP plumbing, remote cron, ...): record no history.
if [[ $- != *i* && -n $SSH_CONNECTION ]]; then
    unset HISTFILE
    return 2>/dev/null || exit
fi
@end example

@table @asis
@item @code{$- != *i*}
True for any shell without interactive mode: @command{sshd} runs one-shot
commands and remote process execution through exactly such shells, and
they source @file{~/.bashrc} even though they never show a prompt.
@item @code{unset HISTFILE}
With no history file recorded, nothing the shell runs is written to
@file{~/.bash_history} (or anywhere else) when it exits.
@item @code{return 2>/dev/null || exit}
@file{~/.bashrc} is being @emph{sourced}, so @code{return} skips the rest
of the file --- including the interactive multi-window
@code{PROMPT_COMMAND} history trick further down.  The @code{|| exit}
fallback covers the corner case of the file being executed rather than
sourced.  Interactive shells ($- contains @code{i}) are unaffected and
keep their normal history behavior.
@end table

You can verify the guard without Emacs: run @kbd{ssh localhost true} a few
times and confirm @file{~/.bash_history} stops growing.  The guard does
not touch existing history files; truncating a @file{~/.tramp_history}
that has already grown is a user-run command, never something this package
executes for you.

@subheading @file{~/.tramp_history}

@cindex TRAMP, remote shell history
@cindex @file{~/.tramp_history}
@cindex @code{tramp-remote-process-environment}
Every shell TRAMP opens on a remote host records its history into
@file{~/.tramp_history} on that host: TRAMP overrides @code{HISTFILE}
itself (@code{tramp-histfile-override}, by default
@file{~/.tramp_history}), so the connection's login shell and every
inner shell spawned for the reads behind the auto-refreshing remote
views append to the same file.  With several cities watched over TRAMP
this can grow to hundreds of kilobytes.

gascity.el points @code{HISTFILE} at the null device for its own remote
buffers: the view buffer factory appends the entry
@code{HISTFILE=/dev/null} to the buffer-local
@code{tramp-remote-process-environment} of every buffer it creates
(the environment TRAMP consults when spawning a remote process from
that buffer).  The entry is appended, never rebound wholesale, so
TRAMP's own default environment entries survive; and the override is
scoped to gascity.el's buffers --- your other TRAMP usage outside
gascity.el keeps the untouched global value.  If you want the
silencing everywhere, set the same entry yourself:

@example
(with-eval-after-load 'tramp
  (add-to-list 'tramp-remote-process-environment
               "HISTFILE=/dev/null"))
@end example

The override does not shrink a @file{~/.tramp_history} that has already
grown.  Truncating it is a user-run command on the remote host ---
never something gascity.el (or any agent or workflow) executes for
you:

@example
: > ~/.tramp_history
@end example

@c ====================================================================
@node Customization
@chapter Customization
@cindex customization

All user options live in the @code{gascity} group; faces live in
@code{gascity-faces}.  Configure them with @kbd{M-x customize-group
@key{RET} gascity @key{RET}} or by setting the variables in your init
file.

@section User options

@defvr {User Option} gascity-executable
Name of, or path to, the @command{gc} executable (default @code{"gc"}).  A
bare command name is resolved against @code{exec-path}; an absolute path
is used as-is.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-enable-debug
When non-@code{nil}, log @command{gc} invocations to the
@file{*gascity-log*} buffer (default @code{nil}).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-debug-level
Verbosity of debug logging when enabled: @code{error}, @code{info}
(default), or @code{verbose} (which also logs command output).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-terminal-backend
Backend used when attaching to an agent's tmux session: @code{nil}
auto-detects (@code{vterm}, then @code{eat}, then the built-in
@code{term}), or force one of @code{vterm}, @code{eat}, @code{term}
(default @code{nil}).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-tmux-socket
The tmux server socket the city's agents run on (@code{tmux -L}).  When
@code{nil} (default) it is auto-detected as the city name; a string
overrides it, and the literal @code{"default"} means the default tmux
server.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-terminal-unshadow-minor-modes
Global minor modes whose keymaps gascity neutralises inside its terminal
buffers, so keys the terminal should own are not swallowed before they
reach it (default @code{(pixel-scroll-precision-mode)}; see @ref{Actions,,
Attached terminals}).  Each mode listed is given an empty keymap in the
buffer's @code{minor-mode-overriding-map-alist} --- buffer-locally, and
never over an entry another package already made.  Set to @code{nil} to
touch no keymaps.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-terminal-mode-line-status
When non-@code{nil} (default), on attaching to an agent gascity turns that
session's own tmux status bar off and mirrors it --- the friendly name and
the window list --- as a buffer-local mode-line segment, so the terminal
shows one status line instead of two.  Reverted when the buffer is killed.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-terminal-preload-idle
Seconds of idle after the first gascity view before the terminal
backend's library (vterm, eat, term) is loaded ahead of the first
attach (default 10).  That load is one indivisible step of a few
hundred milliseconds; it runs only on genuine idle and waits again when
input is pending, so it never lands while you type.  @code{nil}
disables it: the first attach then pays the cost.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-terminal-status-interval
Seconds between refreshes of that mode-line tmux-status segment (default
@code{5}; values at or below zero fall back to 5).
@end defvr

@subheading The cockpit

@defvr {User Option} gascity-dashboard-section-rows
Rows a cockpit section shows before its @samp{@dots{} N more} line
(default 5).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-dashboard-window
The Activity section's time window, a @code{gc events --since} duration
(default @code{"2h"}); @kbd{/ -W} changes it per city.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-dashboard-run-idle-threshold
Seconds without step progress after which an active run is listed in
Needs you (default 1800).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-dashboard-agent-idle-threshold
Seconds of inactivity after which a running agent counts as idle
(default 3600).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-live-enabled
Whether views keep their city's event stream running (default
@code{t}); @kbd{W} flips it per city.  See @ref{Live Refresh} for
@code{gascity-live-debounce}, @code{gascity-live-backoff} and
@code{gascity-live-poll-interval}.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-dashboard-event-levels
Which event types are signals: an alist of (@var{regexp} . @var{level}),
@var{level} @code{attention} (@samp{■}) or @code{watch} (@samp{▲}).
Signal events are never folded into churn.
@end defvr

@subheading Health, Cities and the lighter

@defvr {User Option} gascity-health-doctor-timeout
Seconds before a @command{gc doctor} run is killed (default 300).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-remote-hosts
Remote hosts whose cities the Cities view lists, as TRAMP prefixes
(@code{"/ssh:build-box:"}) or bare host names (meaning
@code{"/ssh:@var{host}:"}).  Hosts visited this session are listed
without being named here.  Default @code{nil}.
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-pulse-store-samples
How many store-size samples to keep per city for Health's sparkline
(default 40).
@end defvr

@subheading Runs

@defvr {User Option} gascity-runs-history-page
Closed runs per history page of the Runs view (default 20).
@end defvr

@defvr {User Option} gascity-runs-failed-window
How far back the Runs view's Failed section looks (default
@code{"24h"}); @kbd{/ -W} overrides it.
@end defvr

@noindent
The remote options --- @code{gascity-remote-transport},
@code{gascity-remote-ssh-options}, @code{gascity-remote-max-inflight},
@code{gascity-remote-async-timeout}, @code{gascity-remote-sync-timeout}
--- are described in @ref{Remote Cities}.

@section Context overrides

@vindex gascity-context-city
@vindex gascity-context-rig
@findex gascity-context-clear-cache
gascity resolves the @dfn{current} city and rig from
@code{default-directory}.  Bind @code{gascity-context-city} to an absolute
path or @code{gascity-context-rig} to a rig name to override that
resolution, and run @kbd{M-x gascity-context-clear-cache} to forget cached
resolutions.

@section Faces

@cindex faces
The @code{gascity-faces} group defines the porcelain's state colors, each
inheriting from a standard face so it tracks your theme:

@table @code
@item gascity-header
Section headers (bold).
@item gascity-city
The city name (keyword face).
@item gascity-rig
A rig name (function-name face).
@item gascity-running
A running agent or service (success).
@item gascity-stopped
A stopped agent (shadow).
@item gascity-suspended
A suspended rig or agent (warning).
@item gascity-warning
A watch state, @samp{▲} (warning).
@item gascity-failed
A failed or errored state (error).
@item gascity-dim
Secondary, de-emphasized text (shadow).
@end table

